0070 - esp32s31 crypto + TRNG: stop fighting hart0 for shared blocks
=====================================================================

Full copies of drivers/crypto/esp32s31-crypto.c and
drivers/char/hw_random/esp32s31-rng.c. Both bugs were found by the ESP-IDF
HEAD review (docs/esp-idf-toolchain-review-2026-09-25.md). They were fixed
2026-09-25 and shipped in kernel #391.

crypto
1. AES and SHA are no longer registered. hart0's mbedtls clears
   HP_SYS_CLKRST crypto_ctrl0 aes_clk_en and sha_clk_en (bits 4 and 6) on
   every release, even with CLK_ON_DEMAND=n. It also pulses their resets on
   every acquire. After hart0's first WPA handshake, a Linux ecb(aes) or
   sha* request therefore polled a gated block. Nothing consumed them: there
   is no AF_ALG user, and BlueZ SMP uses the library AES. The probe no
   longer sets bits 4 and 6. The tables stay in the source, marked
   __maybe_unused.
2. The probe reads the crypto clock source mux (crypto_ctrl0 [21:20],
   2 = PLL_F240M, owned by hart0). If it is anything else, the probe says so
   and returns -ENODEV. It no longer spends 2 s per engine timing out. The
   2026-09-03 boot lost 6.2 s to those timeouts.

   On #391 the mux reads 2 and the probe completes by 4.07 s with no gap in
   the log. The RSA/ECC self-test still FAILS, now with -EIO (a wrong
   answer) rather than -110 (a timeout). So RSA and ECDH are not
   registered either, exactly as before. Nothing on the board uses them.
   That failure is a separate, pre-existing bug and is still open.

rng
3. enable() now sets only the clock and enable bits that are off. It no
   longer pulses RNG_BUS_RST_EN, and the devm teardown that cleared the
   enables is removed. hart0's esp_random() reads the same block, so a
   Linux probe, unbind or reset had been able to stop hart0's entropy
   source (Wi-Fi and BT key generation) underneath it.
